7. Questions your team might ask
ECS Service provides container lifecycle management, desired-count reconciliation, deployment management, and replacement of failed Tasks. EC2 only provides the underlying compute.


=> 9. Closing statement for your presentation
Based on my research, we can migrate our current orchestrator and batch worker architecture to AWS using ECS, EC2, EventBridge Scheduler, and Lambda.

For the orchestrator, the migration is straightforward because we currently maintain only one Pod. An ECS Service with a desired count of one can provide similar behavior.

For batch workers, we can replace KEDA’s MySQL-based scaling mechanism with an EventBridge-triggered Lambda function. Lambda would check the queue, calculate the required number of workers, and launch ECS Tasks on EC2 instances.

The main difference is that KEDA currently provides scaling logic and Kubernetes Job management for us. In AWS, we would need to implement and validate some of that behavior ourselves, especially concurrency control, retries, and failure recovery.

My suggestion would be to start with a proof of concept: deploy one orchestrator ECS Service, configure the worker Task Definition, and build a Lambda that queries our existing MySQL table and launches worker Tasks based on queue demand.

Then we can test the same scenarios we currently support in Kubernetes, including an empty queue, multiple queued batches, concurrent workers, and worker failures, before deciding whether to proceed with the full migration.